{"attack": true, "case": "The target's mechanism is self-undermining: it claims the preference is rational because the best-corroborated theory 'is the most efficient vehicle for further testing' and 'maximizes the chance of quickly falsifying a false theory,' but this very claim requires an inductive bridge from past testing performance to future testing efficiency. If corroboration is merely 'a report of past performance' carrying no future probability (as the target insists), then there is no non-inductive basis for asserting that a theory which survived severe tests yesterday will be an efficient vehicle for testing today. The mechanism thus smuggles in the assumption that past testing history is informative about future falsification speed — the very inductive assumption the target purports to renounce. The target's own countercondition 3 identifies this collapse, but the mechanism does not actually avoid it; it merely relabels the inductive inference ('likely true') as a different inductive inference ('likely efficient for testing').", "premise": "The target's mechanism explicitly grounds rational preference on the claim that the best-corroborated theory maximizes future falsification efficiency, which is a forward-looking probabilistic inference from past corroboration — i.e., induction.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "016208c87024f0535f9a002f53ccfdf4907f5a3a035a5d716fd6961d245d9e7e", "quote": "By choosing the most severely tested theory, scientists maximize the chance of quickly falsifying a false theory, thereby accelerating progress toward truth (verisimilitude) without assuming the future will resemble the past. The preference is rational not because the theory is likely true, but because it is the most efficient vehicle for further testing."}], "counterexample": ["A theory T that survived severe tests yesterday is, by the target's own stipulation, no more likely to survive or behave in any particular way today (corroboration implies no future probability). But if T's past performance carries no information about its future behavior, then there is equaly no reason to believe T will 'maximize the chance of quickly falsifying a false theory' or be 'the most efficient vehicle for further testing' going forward. The efficiency claim is a future-probability claim dressed in methodological language."], "successor_question": "Can a preference be genuinely non-inductive if its rationalizing mechanism depends on the claim that a theory's past testing history is informative about its future testing efficiency?"}